% B SECTION, bars 37-64 (Poco più mosso, 4/4), D flat major.
% 37-44 B1: new theme in flute 1 alone (37-40), then the cor anglais with flute 1 in tenths and sixths (41-44, c021/c090),
%          over viola triplets (pizz. 41-44) and the pizzicato bass with harp.
%          It opens with a rising fourth. c120: bars 42 and 58 are a diatonic turn over ii7 (A flat-B flat-A flat-G flat-F, accepted in cycle 11
%          to reduce the tail count), not the motto's tail; the tail statements are 8, 24, 33, 63 (in the quotation), 68, 72, 85-89.
% 45-52 B2: the theme's head in B flat minor (oboe 1, cellos an octave lower, c041), D flat 7 = C sharp 7, then F sharp minor (= G flat minor):
%          the cor anglais (since c040; oboe 1 plays theme B's B flat minor head at 45-47) quotes the movement I second-theme head
%          in minor with its pickup (cyclic-notes §3), once, 47 b4-50.
%          G sharp 7 respelled A flat 7 leads back to D flat.
% 53-60 B3: the theme in both violin sections (unison at 53-56, octaves from 57), horn 1 counter-melody from the A theme's head;
%          woodwinds and horns join for the crescendo.
% 61-64 B4: climax on D flat (61), G flat to F flat (= E major) (62), then A minor: trombone 1 quotes the
%          movement I first-theme head once, ff, in a foreign key (cyclic-notes: Dvořák 9/ii 96-98 model).

bSecFluteOne = {
  f''2(\p\<^\markup \italic "dolce cantabile" ges''8 f''8 es''4\! | des''4\> c''8 des''8 es''2)\! | ges''4(\< \tuplet 3/2 4 { f''8 ges'' f'' } es''4\> des''4 | c''4\p des''8 c''8 bes'2) \breathe |  % c036: breath between theme B's two halves (Adler part 1 PDF 188). c031: head rhythm 2 8 8 4 at 37/45/53, so theme B does not open with theme A's 4. 8 (Dvorak 9/ii: bar 7 vs bar 46)
  r2 aes''4.(\pp\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." bes''8 | f''4\p ges''8 f''8 es''4~ es''8\> f''8 | ges''4. aes''8 f''4 es''4 | es''2.)\pp r4 |  % c101: one level under the cor anglais (p< mp), so theme B's second phrase, the lower voice of the pair, keeps the lead (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 54: a melody in the middle register needs relief by dynamic shading)  % c090: flute 1 in tenths and sixths above the cor anglais (41-44), not in octaves: the symphony's first melody in parallel thirds/sixths (cyclic-notes, mvt I c187; Dvorak 9/ii 55-56, clarinets in thirds in the middle section; Adler part 2 PDF 257-258: the repeat of a tune in a different colour; PDF 273: Beethoven Op. 61, melody harmonised by paired woodwinds). Was f''4.( ges''8 | aes''4 bes''8 aes''8 ges''4~ ges''8 f''8 | es''4. f''8 des''4 bes'4 | c''2.)  % c051: flute 1 rests 41 b1-2; the cor anglais starts theme B's second phrase alone in its strong low register and the flute joins on F5 (Sebesky PDF 219: compensate for a soloist's weak register; Adler part 1 PDF 185, 205)  % c061: 42 b3-4 the 7th G flat of A flat7 is held into the D flat chord as a 4-3 (A&S PDF 359-360, Ex. 21-23a: dissonant preparation), the theme's only suspension (also 58)
  R1*8 |
  R1*2 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring #"vlnOneQ" #UP { <>^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" R1*2 } } |  % c315: part-only cue before the entry at 57 after 12 bars' rest across poco rit. (47), poco sostenuto (48) and a tempo, poco a poco animando (53) (MOLA PDF 5, Ex. 4, PDF 10)
  % c198: 58 the crescendo from 57 peaks on the suspended G flat (b4, the 4 over D flat) and a short diminuendo takes the resolution F into the breath; 59 f as before; the 58 b4/b4.5 dynamics are hidden, for score.midi only (without them the crescendo overshot the f of 59). Bar 42's 4-3 is shaped the same way (c098). A&S PDF 348: 'dissonant suspensions receive something of an accent and their resolutions are softer'; PDF 357 Ex. 21-17 (Mozart K. 378): cresc. into the 7-6 chain, p where it starts, so the chain is not inside a running crescendo
  \stemDown aes''4(\p\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." des'''4 f'''4. ges'''8 | aes'''4. ges'''8~ ges'''4~ ges'''8-\tweak stencil ##f \mf \> f'''8)-\tweak stencil ##f \mp \breathe | es'''4.(\f f'''8 des'''4 bes''4 | c'''1)\< \breathe |  % c015: Fl 2 rests, so stems follow the notes (Gould PDF 38); the combiner keeps voice one here  % c151: 58 A flat6 dotted quarter, G flat6 from b2.5 tied over b3: the A flat over the ii7 bass E flat now resolves on the weaker half of b2, and G flat (3rd of ii7) is held as the prepared 7th of V7 and the 4-3 over D flat (A&S PDF 347, 357-358, 364, 369)
  % c110: 58 top octave holds A flat6 (was aes'''4 bes'''8 aes'''8): the B section's ceiling stays A flat6/A6 (61-63) and B flat6 is first heard in A' (76, 79), the movement's peak above movement I's A flat6 (A&S PDF 600-601: register and key plan project the motive; Ob 1/Vln II keep the B flat turn an octave lower; the G flat-F 4-3 suspension is unchanged)
  \tag #'score \stemUp \tag #'part \stemNeutral aes'''1--~\ff | aes'''1( | \once \stemDown a'''4)\sf-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." \tweak staff-position #0 r4 \tweak staff-position #0 r2 | R1 |  % c070: the top voice's G sharp6 (A flat6) resolves to A6 in the flute that holds it, with the violins' A6, instead of stopping while the violins take the A (A&S PDF 394, Ex. 23-12: a chromatic succession split between voices)  % c059: flute 1 holds the A flat6 the upper half of Vln I had; flute 2 keeps the augmented head  % c021: a2 with flute 2  % c275: in the score \stemNeutral here undid the part-combiner's up-stems for flute 1 for the rest of the movement (it reverts the override in the combiner's voice), so at 79-80 both flutes took down-stems and Fl 1's black B flat6 and Fl 2's minim F6 shared one stem at 80 b1 (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 60: notes of different duration take separate stems except in string writing; PDF 65: upper part up-stems, minims and black noteheads kept separate); \stemUp restores the combiner's direction, the part keeps \stemNeutral  % c284: sf on the A6 with the strings' sf> (was ff carried over from 61): flute 1 is the only wind left at 63 and its top-register A6 sounded ff over the trombone quotation's first notes (Adler part 2 PDF 147: the instruments not carrying the statement marked down so they do not dominate; PDF 152: the high flute octave pierces without doubling; PDF 156: a new colour set off so it is clearly perceived)
}
% c021: flute 2 joins flute 1 in unison on theme B's augmented head at the climax, so the upper octave of theme B's head (trumpet 1's until c060; trumpets tacet since then; the head now sounds in Fl 2, Vln II and Vc in three octaves, c161/c321)
% A flat-D flat-F-F flat has two flutes (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 75: "in the top register two flutes will suffice").
bSecFluteTwo = {
  R1*8 | R1*8 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { s1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring #"oboeOneQ" #UP { <>^\markup \tiny "Ob. 1" R1*2 } } |  % c155: score spacer, as c115 at 63: the second player's whole rest at 59 sat on player 1's down-stems (Gould PDF 50: rests move clear of the other part; PDF 51: shared rests)  % c135: cue in the part before the entry after a long rest (MOLA PDF 5: logical, audible cues during long rests)
 
  aes''2--\ff des'''2-- | f'''2-- fes'''2-- | \tag #'score s1 \tag #'part R1 | R1 |  % c176: no slur (was aes''2--( ... fes''2--)): the 61-62 wind chord is tongued on every half note by all other winds (Sebesky PDF 23 items 1, 11; Ex. 47, 51)  % c115: in the score flute 2's tacet bar 63 is a spacer, so flute 1's rests are the only ones (Gould PDF 51: parts share a rest wherever possible)
}
% c043: 46 b3-47 a swell to the C sharp 7 (V7 of the F sharp minor quotation): oboe 1 and cellos mf, the accompaniment mp, back to pp
% at 48, so the middle section has a smaller wave before the build at 53-61 (A&S PDF 247-248: the applied dominant reached in a chain of
% descending 5ths is a goal chord; Dvorak 9/ii Largo, corpus MusicXML: f at 48 and 53 in the C sharp minor section before the ff at 96).
bSecOboeOne = {
  R1*5 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring #"corAnglaisQ" #DOWN { <>^\markup \tiny "C.a." R1*2 } } | r2 r4 aes'4(\p-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1."^\markup \italic "dolce espr." |  % c315: part-only cue before the entry at 44 after 21 bars' rest across the poco rit. (35), the metre change and Poco più mosso (37) (MOLA PDF 5, Ex. 4, PDF 10); #DOWN so the oboe's own rest goes above the low cue notes (c165, Gould PDF 50-51)  % c071: pickup A flat4-D flat5 (rising fourth) into the head, alone on the empty fourth beat of 44
  % c041: theme B's head in B flat minor (45-47) moves from Vln I to oboe 1, so each statement of theme B has its own colour
  % (Fl 37, Fl + Eh 41, Ob 45, violins 53, tutti 57) and the oboe, "basically a lyrical instrument", sings one phrase of a theme
  % in its "warm, prominent, reedy and poignant" range F4-C6 (Adler part 1 PDF 198); it hands the C sharp5 to its sister
  % instrument, the cor anglais, whose quotation picks up on 47 b4 (PDF 204-205). Cellos keep the lower octave (c011).
  des''2-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." es''8 des''8 c''4) | bes'4( a'8 bes'8 des''2\<) | cis''2.\mf\> r4\! |
  R1*9 |  % c040: the movement I quotation 47-53 is in the cor anglais (see bSecCorAnglais)
  % c198: 58 the crescendo from 57 peaks on the suspended G flat (b4, the 4 over D flat) and a short diminuendo takes the resolution F into the breath; 59 f as before; the 58 b4/b4.5 dynamics are hidden, for score.midi only (without them the crescendo overshot the f of 59). Bar 42's 4-3 is shaped the same way (c098). A&S PDF 348: 'dissonant suspensions receive something of an accent and their resolutions are softer'; PDF 357 Ex. 21-17 (Mozart K. 378): cresc. into the 7-6 chain, p where it starts, so the chain is not inside a running crescendo
  \stemDown aes'4(\p\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." des''4 f''4. ges''8 | aes''4. ges''8~ ges''4~ ges''8-\tweak stencil ##f \mf \> f''8)-\tweak stencil ##f \mp \breathe | es''4.(\f f''8 des''4 bes'4 | c''1)\< \breathe |  % c119: 58 holds A flat5 like Fl 1/Vln I's A flat6 (c110), so the two octaves of theme B match; B flat waits for A'  % c151: as Fl 1 at 58 (A&S PDF 347, 357-358)  % c166: breath after 58 with flute 1 and horn 1; 57-60 was about 15.5 s in one breath, p to f (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 37, 44)
  \tag #'score \stemUp f''1--~\ff | f''2 fes''2-- \tag #'score \stemNeutral | R1*2 |  % c255: c014's up-stems are for the shared score staff only and end after 62; the Oboe 1 part had all stems up at 61-62 and through 79-82 (Gould PDF 35, 38)  % c014: oboe 1 above the trumpets at 61 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 110-111)  % c176: F5 tied into 62 like clarinet 1's unison F5 (was slurred tenutos, re-articulated against the clarinet's tie; Sebesky PDF 23, 80)
}
bSecOboeTwo = {
  R1*8 | R1*8 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { s1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring #"oboeOneQ" #UP { <>^\markup \tiny "Ob. 1" R1*2 } } |  % c155: score spacer, as c115 at 63: the second player's whole rest at 59 sat on player 1's down-stems (Gould PDF 50: rests move clear of the other part; PDF 51: shared rests)  % c135: cue in the part before the entry after a long rest (MOLA PDF 5: logical, audible cues during long rests)
 
  des''1--~\ff | des''2 aes'2-- | R1*2 |  % c176: D flat5 tied into 62 with oboe 1's F5, so the shared chord-stem tie is unambiguous (Vln I also holds D flat6)  % c086: A flat4, not F flat5 in unison with oboe 1 (Sebesky PDF 71, 80). c047: D flat5 held at 61 (was A flat4-D flat5 with trumpet 1, clarinet 1, horn 3 and Vln II): with oboe 1's F5 and the flutes' A flat5 the double reeds complete the D flat chord above the trumpets (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 110-111), filling A flat4-F5. c014: was F4 = Tpt 2 + Hn 2
}
% c009: the lower octave of theme B goes to the cor anglais (Rimsky-Korsakov Ex. 57: Fl / Eng. horn in octaves).
% c021: 37-40 flute 1 alone (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 66-67: an intimate melody to a solo instrument of simple timbre;
% doubling where the expression broadens); the lower octave would sit in unison with the Vln II chord tones F4, D flat4, G flat4.
% The cor anglais joins at 41 with the crescendo.
bSecCorAnglais = {
  f''8) r8 r4 r2 | R1*3 |  % c271: F5 an eighth (was a quarter): it still coincides with flute 1's first note (c139's rule), but the cor anglais, at written C6 in its upper register, colours theme B's first note for half a beat instead of a whole beat; in a soft flute + double-reed unison the reed predominates in its upper register (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 65 a), and the tune's first statement belongs to the flute's simple timbre (PDF 66-67)  % c139: the pickup C5 goes on to F5 in unison with flute 1's first note, so theme B's rising fourth is not split between two instruments (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 125-126: in a phrase transferred between instruments the last note of one coincides with the first of the next)
  aes4(\p\<^\markup \italic "solo, dolce cantabile" des'4 f'4. ges'8 | aes'4\mp bes'8 aes'8 ges'4~ ges'8\> f'8 | es'4. f'8 des'4 bes4 | c'2.)\p r4 |
  % c040: the quotation of movement I's second-theme head (minor, with pickup), once, moved here from oboe 1. Its pickup C sharp4 lay in the
  % oboe's lowest fifth, where Adler (part 1 PDF 198) says it "honks" and should not be asked to play softly; C sharp4-D5 is the cor anglais'
  % middle register (Adler part 2 PDF 188: the solo instrument is chosen for the tune's register and dynamic). In movement I the cor anglais
  % plays the motto with pickup at 5-8 and 171-174, whose first bar is the first bar of this quotation (Adler part 1 PDF 19: timbre
  % clarifies content). Pitches, rhythm, dynamics and "espr." as in v40.
  R1*2 | r2 r4 \set Staff.midiMinimumVolume = #0.15 \set Staff.midiMaximumVolume = #0.9 cis'4(\p^\markup \italic "solo, espr." | a'2.~ a'8 gis'8 | fis'4 d''2--\< cis''4\! ) | cis''2(\mp \tag #'score \once \stemUp b'4 a'4) \breathe |  % c048: tenuto and swell on the syncopated D5, as on the S head's syncopated half note in every movement I statement (mvt I tTwo 32-33, dev 97-99, recTwo 79, coda 69; A&S PDF 51: a note held from a weak through a strong beat conflicts with the meter)
  aes'2(\> ges'2 | f'2. ges'4)\p |
  f'2\> \unset Staff.midiMinimumVolume \unset Staff.midiMaximumVolume r2\! | R1*3 |  % c240: MIDI only, 47 b4-53: the equalizer's cor anglais range (0-0.7, c228) rendered the printed p quotation at velocity 37-44 under the pp cellos and basses (50) and Vln II (41); 0.15-0.9 renders it one level above them, as printed (Sebesky PDF 240-241: compare the score with the recorded result) R1*3 |
  R1*8 |
}
bSecClOne = {
  R1*8 |
  R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring #"corAnglaisQ" #UP c' { <>^\markup \tiny "C.a." R1*2 } } |  % c285: part-only cue before the entry at 53 after 52 bars' rest (MOLA PDF 5, Ex. 4, PDF 10: logical, audible cues during long rests, in the reader's key: genparts' \transpose bes c' does not reach quoted notes but does transpose this pitch argument, so c' prints the cue at written pitch)
  r2 r4 aes8(\pp bes8 | des'4.\< f'8 \tag #'score \once \stemUp bes'2~ | \tag #'score \once \stemUp bes'2 ges'2\p | c'2 des'2) \breathe |  % c201: as horn 1  % c038: one level under horn 1, so the unison is one colour (Casanelles PDF 147-148). c019: unison with Hn 1's counter-line, which the viola triplets cover in the same register  % c051: 56 C4-D flat4 (was E flat4-D flat4): the counter-line rises against the theme's C5-B flat4 instead of following it in sixths after its G flat-F-E flat-D flat descent (Sebesky PDF 211-212, Ex. 279-280)
  \partCombineApart r2 des''2~(\mp\< | des''2 c''4 des''4~ | des''2)\f r2 | c''1\< \breathe |  % c049: rests at 59 b3-4 (was E flat5, held over the theme's lower octave D flat5-B flat4 in Ob 1 and Vln II; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 133, 157); both clarinets re-enter on the V7 swell at 60  % c047: 57 b3-59 an octave higher, above horn 1: the clarinets fill the empty F4-A flat5 space under the theme's lower octave instead of crowding A flat3-F4 with the violas and bassoon (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108: a horn chord is completed by clarinets in the upper register, clarinets rarely inside the horns; PDF 108-109: forte, double the upper harmonic parts); also no E flat4-C5 leap into 60  % c045: apart mode 57-60 (no a2 label on the single unison at 57 b3)  % c147: rest at 57 b1-2 (was F4, with clarinet 2's D flat4): the clarinets enter on b3 above horn 1, so they complete the chord in the upper register from their first note and no longer double the viola triplets' D flat4/F4 under the horn (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108)
  \partCombineAutomatic f''1--\ff~ | f''2 aes''2-- | R1*2 |  % c167: 62 b1-2 F5 held (was G flat5): G flat5 over Ob 1/Vln II F5 was the movement's only adjacent second above E5 (Sebesky PDF 139, rule 5, Ex. 190-192: seconds strident as the chord rises, keep them in the lower-middle register); the G flat-F major seventh stays in Hn 2 G flat4 over Vc/Bsn 1 F4  % c084: clarion F5-G flat5-A flat5 (written G5-A flat5-B flat5), following oboe 1's C5-F5 and doubling horn 2 / trombone 2 two octaves up; was A flat4-D flat5 | B flat4-C flat5, now in clarinet 2 (Casanelles PDF 257, 275: each register its own strength; PDF 277-278: a weak unison doubling adds nothing audible; Adler part 1 PDF 210: throat F4-A flat4 'rather pale')  % c014: clarinets with the trumpets in unison, under the oboes (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 106; natural order PDF 67)
}
bSecClTwo = {
  R1*8 | R1*8 |
  R1*2 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring #"clOneQ" #UP c' { <>^\markup \tiny "Cl. 1" R1*2 } } |  % c285: part-only cue before the entry at 57 after 50 bars' rest (MOLA PDF 5, Ex. 4)
  \partCombineApart r2 bes'2~(\mp\< | bes'2 aes'4 ces''4 | bes'2)\f r2 | aes'1\< \breathe |  % c049: rests at 59 b3-4 (was D flat5, over the theme's B flat4 at b4); breath before the swell at 60  % c047: 57 b3-59 an octave higher with clarinet 1 (see there); B flat4 held into 58 (vi7), no B flat-A flat octaves with the violas' arpeggio bass; C flat5 (7th of V7/IV) resolves to B flat4 at 59  % c032: A flat4, not a second C under the melody's leading tone before the D flat climax (A&S PDF 266, 286); resolves to F4
  \partCombineAutomatic aes'2--\ff des''2-- | bes'2-- ces''2-- | R1*2 |  % c084: clarinet 1's former line (A flat4 held from 60, then clarion D flat5, B flat4-C flat5 with horn 3); was F4 | G flat4-A flat4, all throat tones in unison with horn 2  % c014: was F3 inside the trombones
}
bSecBassCl = {
  des2)\p\> r2\! | R1*7 |  % c140: D flat3 (was D flat4, with Vln II's D flat4), the end of the lowered solo
  R1*8 | R1*2 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring #"vlnOneQ" #UP c { <>^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" R1*2 } } |  % c315: part-only cue before the entry at 57 after 19 bars' rest and four tempo words (47, 48, 53) (MOLA PDF 5, Ex. 4); c = genparts' \transpose bes, c' gives d', so the violins' G flat5-B flat4 print as A flat5-C5 (an octave below the clarinet's reading pitch, inside the stave and clear of the player's own rest)
  f2\p\< ges2 | bes,4 es4 aes,4 \once \stemUp des4 | ges,2\f g,2 | aes,1\< \breathe |  % c033: enters p and catches up by 59 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 127). c017: takes the cellos' bass line 57-60
  des1--\ff | ges,2-- fes,2-- | R1*2 |
}
bSecBsnOne = {
  f2\pp-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." r2 | R1*7 |  % c345: "1." restated at the new system; the combiner printed it at 35 only (Adler part 2 PDF 295)
  % c087: bassoon 1 doubles the cellos' lower octave of theme B's head under oboe 1 (45-47). Oboe over cellos alone was strings and wood-wind in separate
  % octaves, which Rimsky-Korsakov's editor calls "not to be recommended, as the tone quality of the two groups is so widely different" (PDF 78 note);
  % with the bassoon the wood-wind runs in octaves and only the lower part is doubled by a string group (PDF 78), and "a bassoon with the 'cellos" is his
  % model of an equal-power unison in which the strings still predominate (PDF 76). Dynamics as bassoon 2 (shared staff). No pickup at 44: the bassoon staff is hidden in 41-44.
  des'2(\pp es'8 des'8 c'4) | bes4( a8 bes8 des'2\<) | cis'2.\p\> r4\! | R1*5 |
  R1 | des1~(\pp | des2 c2 | ges2 f2) |  % c107: enters at 54 under the harp's arpeggio (Adler part 1 PDF 136: Brahms 3/iii, the bassoons 'clarify and support the arpeggiated harmony'; PDF 143, Ex. V-34: sustained harmony under an arpeggiated figure); D flat3 = 5th of IV6, 7th of ii6/5 and ii7, held to 55 b3  % c042: G flat3 = the 7th of A-C-E flat-G flat (vii°7 of B flat minor) at 56, upper neighbour to F as Vln II at 39-40 (A&S PDF 377, Ex. 22-6a); d7 A2-G flat3 resolves to P5 B flat2-F3 (PDF 381 box)
  % c145: 57-58 bassoon 2 alone with a score-only "2." label; bassoon 1 has spacers in the score, rests in its part (was r1 r1 in bassoon 1, which drew a whole rest on a ledger line above the staff at 57 and no player label; Adler part 2 PDF 294: each instrument labelled when it enters; App. A PDF 312: "1., 2.")
  \partCombineApart \tag #'score { s1*2 } \tag #'part { R1*2 } | ges2\f es4( fes4 | es4\< f4 ges2) \breathe |  % c117: 60 follows the counter-line with Hn 1 and Vc (was es1): the line keeps its woodwind-brass-string colour into 61 (Adler 2 PDF 176: keep an established colour long enough; PDF 178: string + woodwind + brass per element)  % c057: rests 57-58 (was F3-B flat3 | F3-E flat3-E flat3-F3): at 57 b1 F3 was in four parts (B.cl., Bsn 1-2, Vc) and at 58 bassoon 1 re-struck the cello/horn counter-line's notes (Sebesky PDF 12-13, Ex. 1: economy; lighten the lower-middle register so the primary line dominates); joins the counter-line at the f of 59  % c045: apart mode 57-60: each unison prints as one note head with two stems, no single-beat a2 labels (MOLA Ex. 6 PDF 11)  % c172: 60 b3-4 G flat held (was G flat4. F8): the F anticipation sounded a semitone under trombone 2's G flat3 and the violas' G flat4 in the same octaves; the 7th now resolves at 61 in Tbn 2, Bsn 1, Hn 2 and the violas (A&S PDF 469, 475; Ex. 27-6a)
  \partCombineAutomatic f2--\ff des'2-- | f'2-- fes'2-- | R1*2 |  % c087: F3 on 61 b1-2 (E flat3-A flat3 here made fifths with bassoon 2 and the bass, A flat-D flat), then the augmented head with the cellos from 61 b3 (was F3 | G flat3-A flat3, a unison with trombone 2, also in horn 2 and clarinet 1): the head's lowest octave was the cellos alone among the brass, and strings and brass "can never yield such a perfect blend" (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 79); 'Cellos + Fag. is his natural unison (PDF 76-77), and a melody spread over three octaves is hard to balance (PDF 76)
}
bSecBsnTwo = {
  R1*8 |
  bes,1(\pp | bes,2-- bes,2--\< | \after 2. \pp gis,1)\p\> |  % c126: one breath, repeated B flat soft-tongued (Adler part 1 PDF 177, Ex. VI-7)
  R1 | R1*4 |  % c108: winds p, strings fp on the C sharp 7 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 129)
  R1*4 |  % c077: rests 53-56 (was des2 c2 | bes,2 ges,2 | es2 des2 | a,2 bes,4 r4, the cellos' line at pitch): a wind doubling strings at the unison "detracts from the clarity of a line" (Adler part 1 PDF 265-266); the woodwind bass enters fresh at 57 with B.cl., so the choirs are heard apart, then together (PDF 253)
  \partCombineSoloII f2\p\< ges2 | bes,4 es4 aes,4 \tweak Stem.direction #UP des4 | \partCombineApart ges,2\f  % c205: 57-58 bassoon 2 alone in solo mode (normal stems, "2." printed by the combiner); apart from 59 (Gould PDF 60, 65)
  g,2 | aes,1\< \breathe |
  \partCombineAutomatic des1--\ff | ges,2-- fes,2-\tweak padding #0.6 -- | R1*2 |  % c065: tenuto clear of the stem end (Gould PDF 55)
}
