bSecHnOne = {
  R1*8 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring #"corAnglaisQ" #UP { <>^\markup \tiny "C.a." R1*2 } } |  % c075: cue in the part (MOLA PDF 5, Ex. 4)
  r2 r4 aes8(\pp-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1."^\markup \italic "espr." bes8 | des'4.\< f'8 \tag #'score \once \stemUp bes'2~ | \tag #'score \once \stemUp bes'2 ges'2\mp | c'2 des'2) \breathe |  % c201: B flat4 tied over into 55 (was G flat4 for the whole bar): the held G flat4 sat an octave under the theme's G flat5-F5 oscillation (P8-M7-P8-M7); B flat4 makes a 6th with the theme's G flat5, G flat4 then a 6th under its E flat5; the tie gives the counter-line theme A's tied head rhythm (A&S PDF 39 sec. 8: imperfect consonances between the prominent lines between articulations)  % c019: crescendo with the violins; Cl 1 doubles (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 74-75: wind + horn in unison, finer legato)  % c051: 56 C4-D flat4 (was E flat4-D flat4): the counter-line rises against the theme's C5-B flat4 instead of following it in sixths after its G flat-F-E flat-D flat descent (Sebesky PDF 211-212, Ex. 279-280)
  \partCombineSoloI aes'2(\mp\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." ges'2 | f'4 es'2~ es'8 f'8) \breathe | ges'2\f es'4( fes'4 | es'4\< f'4 ges'2) \breathe |  % c158: G flat detached, slur from E flat, as Bsn 1 and the cellos play the same line (Sebesky PDF 213-215, Ex. 281: one figure, one articulation in every part)  % c082: 58 b4 A flat4, not F4: F was the resolution of the melody's G flat 4-3 suspension sounding under it (A&S PDF 469: suspensions are clearest when the tone of resolution is not in another voice; 42 is already clean); 60 b4 the F is an eighth-note anticipation, so it no longer sits a beat against the violas' G flat (A&S PDF 475)  % c046: breath after 58 (Adler part 1 PDF 292: a loud slurred phrase in a slow tempo needs more breaths; PDF 309: one slur = one breath; matches the cellos' bow change). c025: horn 2 tacet 57-60; force solo so its rests are not printed under horn 1 (Gould PDF 51: parts share a rest)  % c151: 58 b4 E flat4 held to b4.5 (was F4 on b4): the F no longer sounds under the melody's G flat-F suspension, and the held E flat is oblique to the bass A flat-D flat, so no fifths (A&S PDF 352, 356-359, 365-366; c082, c089)  % c172: 60 b3-4 G flat held (was G flat4. F8): the F anticipation sounded a semitone under trombone 2's G flat3 and the violas' G flat4 in the same octaves; the 7th now resolves at 61 in Tbn 2, Bsn 1, Hn 2 and the violas (A&S PDF 469, 475; Ex. 27-6a)
  \partCombineAutomatic f'1--\ff | ges'2-- aes'2-- | R1*2 |  % c264: horns 1 and 2 divided at 61-62 (horn 2 D flat4 | D flat4 F flat4), so the brass chord is in close order: A flat3/F3 (Tbn 2), D flat4, F4, A flat4 at 61; B flat3, D flat4, G flat4, B flat4 and A flat3, F flat4, A flat4, C flat5 at 62 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 100: brass parts in close order with no empty spaces; PDF 103: in a widely-spread chord the horns' register need not be doubled, and single horns are marked one degree louder). Horn 1 keeps its line and the G flat4-F4 resolution (c194).  % c194: horn 1 a2 with horn 2 at 61-62, so every horn part of the climax chord is a pair (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102: 'when the whole group is used the number of horns should be doubled'; PDF 103: the one-degree-louder marking is the fallback when pairs are impossible) and horn 1's 7th G flat4 resolves to F4 in its own line; 63-66 (about 16 s) still rest before the p solo at 67 (Adler part 1 PDF 303: periodic rests after high-register playing; F4-A flat4 is the middle register)  % was R1*4 (c046)  % c075: in horn 1's part the crescendo of 60 ended nowhere and ran through the rests to 65; part-only, so the combiner is unchanged  % c046: horn 1 rests 61-66 before its exposed p solo at 67 (Adler part 1 PDF 292, 303: rests for the lips; the high line goes to horn 3, a high horn)  % (superseded by c264) c194: horn 1 a2 with horn 2 at 61-62, so every horn part of the climax chord is a pair (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102: 'when the whole group is used the number of horns should be doubled'; PDF 103: the one-degree-louder marking is the fallback when pairs are impossible) and horn 1's 7th G flat4 resolves to F4 in its own line; 63-66 (about 16 s) still rest before the p solo at 67 (Adler part 1 PDF 303: periodic rests after high-register playing; F4-A flat4 is the middle register)  % was R1*4 (c046)  % c075: in horn 1's part the crescendo of 60 ended nowhere and ran through the rests to 65; part-only, so the combiner is unchanged  % c046: horn 1 rests 61-66 before its exposed p solo at 67 (Adler part 1 PDF 292, 303: rests for the lips; the high line goes to horn 3, a high horn)
}
% c024: horn 2's first entry is the ff of 61, not mp at 57 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 136: brass re-entries pp or ff; mp/mf "colourless");
